  "baseline_spec": {
    "name": "wild-pear",
    "notes": "AUTHORED IN FLOWER, like `cherry-blossom`, because a wild pear out of flower is a small brown tree and in flower it is the brightest object in a hedge line. `leaf_blossom` at coverage 0.95 and separation 1.45 -- the densest crown in this pass -- so it reads as one solid white mass rather than as foliage that happens to be pale.\n\nThe shape is the other half, and it is the OPPOSITE of the crab apple built beside it: narrow and upright (crown radius 1.7 m on 8 m of height, growth lifting at 0.10) where the crab apple is low and spreading. Two small rose-family trees in one hedge have to differ in silhouette or they are one tree twice. The thorns are spur shoots finer than the lattice and are not attempted.",
